<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Imagawa Yoshimoto</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1519">1519</a>–<a href="/years/1560">1560</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Imagawa Yoshimoto was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sengoku-period">Sengoku</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> who ruled Suruga, Tōtōmi and Mikawa from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunpu">Sunpu</a>, one of the most powerful lords of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. Marching towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> in 1560, he was killed in a surprise attack by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a>'s much smaller force at Okehazama. His death led to the decline of the Imagawa and the rise of Nobunaga.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/imagawa-yoshimoto.jpg" alt="Imagawa Yoshimoto" width="320" height="360" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ImagawaYoshimoto.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Imagawa Yoshimoto was born in 1519, the third son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imagawa-ujichika">Imagawa Ujichika</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imagawa-clan">Imagawa clan</a>, which claimed descent from Emperor Seiwa. As a younger son he was sent to a temple, where he was named Baigaku Shōhō. In 1536 his elder brother Ujiteru died suddenly and a succession dispute followed. His half-brother Genkō Etan sought the lordship, and the clan split. Yoshimoto's faction argued that his mother, Jukei-ni, had been Ujichika's consort. With help from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hojo-ujitsuna">Hōjō Ujitsuna</a> of Sagami and the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takeda-nobutora">Takeda Nobutora</a> of Kai, the other faction was eliminated in the Hanagura Disturbance, and Yoshimoto took his name and the headship.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Yoshimoto married a sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takeda-shingen">Takeda Shingen</a>, which sealed an alliance with the Takeda, and in 1540 he helped Shingen imprison Nobutora. He defeated an invasion of Suruga by the Later Hōjō clan. His advance into Mikawa met a defeat at the 1542 Battle of Azukizaka, but in 1548 he beat Oda Nobuhide in the Second Battle of Azukizaka and gained Suruga, Tōtōmi and Mikawa. Marriages tied him to the Takeda and the Hōjō, and a peace with the Hōjō followed in 1554. He was known as the number one archer in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokaido">Tōkaidō</a>. Later he left the clan's political affairs to his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imagawa-ujizane">Imagawa Ujizane</a> to concentrate on the advance westward into Mikawa and Owari.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In the summer of 1560 Yoshimoto marched towards the capital with Matsudaira Motoyasu, later known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>, in the vanguard. He had a force of 25,000 but announced 40,000, and some sources support the larger figure. After taking several Oda castles his army feasted with song and sake. Following a downpour, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> attacked by surprise with 3,000 men. Two Oda samurai, Mōri Shinsuke and Hattori Koheita, killed Yoshimoto at Dengakuhazama on 12 June. Ujizane succeeded him, but the Imagawa fell from power. Yoshimoto's body is buried at the Daisei-ji temple in Toyokawa, in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aichi-prefecture">Aichi Prefecture</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Imagawa Yoshimoto” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son <b>Yoshimoto</b> made Sunpu a cultured capital, took control of Mikawa, held the young Tokugawa Ieyasu as a hostage and allied with the Takeda and Later Hojo, becoming the strongest lord on the Tokaido.</blockquote><a href="/state/imagawa-clan-domain">Imagawa clan domain</a></li><li><blockquote>His surprise victory over <b>Imagawa Yoshimoto</b> at Okehazama in 1560 made his name, and he took Mino in 1567, adopting a seal proclaiming rule of the realm by force.</blockquote><a href="/state/oda-regime">Oda regime</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Imagawa Yoshimoto's</b> death at Okehazama in 1560 he returned to Okazaki, broke with the Imagawa and allied with Oda Nobunaga in 1562.</blockquote><a href="/state/tokugawa-clan-domain">Tokugawa clan domain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Toyoake, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.05,137.01&amp;year=1545" aria-label="Toyoake on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Imagawa Yoshimoto"><image href="/maps/globes/g150n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4099 4517h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4099 4517h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=35.05,137.01&amp;year=1545">What was here in 1545? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
